{
    "word": "downward",
    "lang_code": "en",
    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "67bf81df-89ac-3727-b0bb-4b644ec89466",
                "word": "downward",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "60c04f45-6dfb-3c78-8436-f26dbcc25e0f",
                        "entry_id": "67bf81df-89ac-3727-b0bb-4b644ec89466",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Moving, sloping or oriented downward."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "12f88497-7770-4f66-bd88-3ab58832e4aa",
                                "sense_id": "60c04f45-6dfb-3c78-8436-f26dbcc25e0f",
                                "text": "He spoke with a downward glance."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7db08b67-44aa-4afd-b773-0307cd82ba5b",
                                "sense_id": "60c04f45-6dfb-3c78-8436-f26dbcc25e0f",
                                "text": "But this foul, grim, and urchin-snouted boar,\nWhose downward eye still looketh for a grave,\nNe’er saw the beauteous livery that he wore;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8a0ac77d-1679-451e-be61-cd6697266330",
                                "sense_id": "60c04f45-6dfb-3c78-8436-f26dbcc25e0f",
                                "text": "[…] in the Western Sky, the downward Sun\nLooks out illustrious from amid the Flush\nOf broken Clouds […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "21437059-4ccc-43a3-881e-b93423054eb4",
                                "sense_id": "60c04f45-6dfb-3c78-8436-f26dbcc25e0f",
                                "text": "Emerging into the hill-road, Kemp naturally took the downward direction […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5a728e16-a5a5-49a7-9ebf-03e86eb408dd",
                                "sense_id": "60c04f45-6dfb-3c78-8436-f26dbcc25e0f",
                                "text": "[…] Therese saw a downward slant of sadness in her mouth now, a sadness not of wisdom but of defeat."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "2df6aaf5-38b3-4ab6-b388-a713d23c5dbf",
                                "sense_id": "60c04f45-6dfb-3c78-8436-f26dbcc25e0f",
                                "text": "The tractive and braking forces are transmitted to the body through a downward projecting pivot pin in the normal way."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "7d70bc53-cf33-3b05-9ea2-efe58aafbd16",
                        "entry_id": "67bf81df-89ac-3727-b0bb-4b644ec89466",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Located at a lower level."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "not-comparable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "305f8190-9563-48ee-bb10-1fffe33ce1b6",
                                "sense_id": "7d70bc53-cf33-3b05-9ea2-efe58aafbd16",
                                "text": "In her chast Current oft the Goddess laves,\nAnd with Celestial Tears augments the Waves.\nOft in her Glass the musing Shepherd spies\nThe headlong Mountains and the downward Skies,\nThe watry Landskip of the pendant Woods,\nAnd absent Trees that tremble in the Floods;"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "4d499230-6ea2-4709-bfe6-524d92dccd9f",
                                "sense_id": "7d70bc53-cf33-3b05-9ea2-efe58aafbd16",
                                "text": "1793, Thomas Taylor (translator), The Phædo in The Cratylus, Phædo, Parmenides and Timæus of Plato, London: Benjamin and John White, p. 235,\n[…] often revolving itself under the earth, [the river] flows into the more downward parts of Tartarus."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "49e899de-6881-40a2-81c9-4974bba33c1a",
